    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Pressure buildup analysis has become an increasingly important facet of reservoir engineering in the last 30 years. Estimates of important reservoir characteristics such as permeability, porosity and compressibility can be made using methods of analysis in existence.One of the first efforts to relate pressure data to reservoir parameter was done by Muskat in 1937. His work was a qualitative analysis because the system compressibility was not taken into account. In 1950, Miller, Dyes, and Hutchinson* (MDH) carried Muskat's analysis a little further to systems with different outer boundary conditions and to systems with closed outer boundary. For their solutions the well was at steady state prior to shut-in. Horner developed a method for infinite systems and plotted the dimensionless pressure buildup against log (t + A t )/a t ) which can be extrapolated to infinite shut-in time. Matthews, Brons, and Hazebroek' (MBH) determined the pressure behavior of a well producing from a bounded area for a variety of shapes and well locations.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
